ppointment i said that's  the most  wonderful news and he said wonderful and  i said yeah i said you came to tell me  that you can't be involved in that  and that means that that boy belongs to  me  and then sure enough later they wanted  to make him stick around here  and then sure enough later he won the  professor of the year  he is a golden young man ben whalen  professor of the year  thank you dr arn that's a very kind  introduction  um i apologize to you all i as i stepped  on the platform my glasses broke  it seems somehow fitting in 2020 to  give an address to an audience i can't  see with a text i can't read  on june 12 1592  when the budding young actor shakespeare  was just 28 years old  and fresh on the scene of london a riot  rocked the english capital  apparently an apprentice to a felt maker  had been arrested  and thrown in prison under dubious  circumstances  his fellow felt-making apprentices  gathered in the southern district to  protest  it was an area pocketed with taverns and  theaters  and was where shakespeare performed the  protest drew a large crowd  and became violent supported as the  mayor of london described things by  quote a great number of loose and  masterless men apt for such purposes  as riot the mayor  william webb responded with vigor  arresting the principal and violent  actors of mariah  the elizabethan government was made  uneasy however  and when the rumor spread that the felt  maker apprentices  were planning a second tumult the privy  council  deployed an armed watch for bad certain  forms of public gathering  and shut down the theaters for three  months it was a hard blow for those who  had made the theater their profession  indeed the suspension of the theaters  lasted far longer  than the initially planned three months  in the  later in the year a large outbreak of  the bubonic plague swept through  england and as the government tried to  limit the spread of the plague  it forced the theaters to remain closed  for much of the next year and a half  what was shakespeare to do here was a  young man  just come to london just beginning his  career  facing a country convulsed by riots and  a deadly disease  shakespeare did what any of us would  have done in such a situation  he organized a zoom rendition of hamlet  notably with his means of employment  gone shakespeare turned to his poetry  producing the only narrative poems that  he ever wrote  venus and adonis and the rape of lucrece  both of these are some of the very  finest narrative poems  in our literary tradition being as an  adonis a playful meditation  on love and desire while the rape of  lucretia is a serious examination  of the nature of suffering the abuse of  power  the foundations of human government and  ancient rome's transition  from monarchy to republic we probably  would be willing enough  to excuse shakespeare had the plague  with its many deadly terrors and surely  daily nuisances and worries occupied all  his attention  and prevented him from writing or at  least writing well  we probably would be willing enough to  excuse shakespeare had his imagination  been occupied by visions of hordes of  felt making apprentices pouring down the  streets  felt in hand ready to light it and watch  london burn  [Music]  indeed it is rather remarkable that  although waves of the bubonic plague  swept through  england several times during  shakespeare's youth and adulthood  for instance he was the year he was born  it killed one out of every five people  in his hometown of stratford  one out of five shakespeare rarely  mentions it  in his works it is surprising  even perhaps a little odd to find how  little notice  how little attention shakespeare seems  to give  to this deadly disease it's almost as if  shakespeare  is calling us to look at other things  higher things  than the banal facts of disease and  death  and it's not that shakespeare is an  escapist in his poetry  nor is he a blinded idealist as i  mentioned  he wrote about suffering extensively but  shakespeare  is less interested in all the different  things that might cause mankind  misery or death and is more interested  in how we might suffer or die well  how we might find meaning or redemption  or grace  in the concrete fact that we all will  suffer  and are bound to become dust faced with  that grim reality  shakespeare turns to the higher things  to search out the springs of mercy and  justice  of love and nobility of sin and  forgiveness  but if shakespeare confronted with civil  disorder  and terrible disease was still able to  turn to higher things  perhaps that was simply because he was  shakespeare a genius unequaled in our  tradition  perhaps in his greatness we can't find  an example for ourselves  let me tell a second story that i take  from james shapiro's book  shakespeare in america in january  1846 part of the us army  found itself stationed in corpus christi  texas  to prepare for the outbreak of the  mexican-american war  corpus christi was described by one  officer as  quote the most murderous thieving  gambling cut throat god forsaken hole in  texas  and if you know texas that's saying a  lot  the troops were growing idol and  idleness and god forsaken holes  are not a good match to counteract this  john b magruder had the soldiers build a  large theater  and then organized them for the  production of several plays  shakespeare's othello among them in the  initial casting  the part of desdemona othello's  beautiful wife  was given to james longstreet the man  whom robert e lee would soon call his  old war horse  long street was a big man standing at  six foot two  more than 200 pounds and with a long  beard  they decided he didn't look quite right  for desdemona  and instead gave the part to the shorter  lighter  ulysses s grant  grant however was too bristly and the  soldier playing othello  grew frustrated complaining quote i  cannot pump up any sentiment with grant  dressed up as desdemona  they eventually sent to have a  professional actress come  and so resolved the difficulty the long  street  did note in his memoirs that grant  looked very like a girl dressed up but  he did not have much sentiment  now i admit that i share this story in  part  because i simply delight in the thought  of ulysses s grant  future president of the united states in  a  playing the gentle elegant desdemona  but the more i've thought about it the  more i've understood  the significance of this action magruder  long street grant all tough men  men of action men preparing for bloody  deeds  men who would play decisive roles in the  terrible conflict  that would determine the course of our  great nation's history  when caught in a god-forsaken hole  trying to manage soldiers and prepare  for a war  they too turned to the higher things to  the fine  and delicate domestic tragedy of othello  this wasn't just busy work for idle  hands  they could have made the soldiers dig  ditches easily enough or any other form  of manual labor  no magruder longstreet and grant were  engaged in a much harder toil  of civilizing men of building culture  of taking the finest things that have  been handed down to us  and making them live again even in texas  but if shakespeare's london or grants  corpus christi  seem too far away from us we are at  hillsdale blessed  to have a long and noble history that we  might look to as well  we all know of course our college's  great triumph during the american civil  war  when our young men went to fight for our  union and when our college nevertheless  continued its mission  to furnish all persons who wish  irrespective of  nation color or sex a literary  scientific  and theological education these are the  higher things  and our college followed its mission  even in the darkest days of our country  but given our current situation my  thoughts turned  to the hillsdale college of 1918  in the spring of that year with world  war one raging  the united states government established  the student army training corps  a program where male students at liberal  arts colleges across the country  were enlisted in a program jointly  administered by the government  and the colleges in which the young men  received both military training  and a liberal arts education the men  were formally enlisted as privates in  the u.s  army and subject to military law  additionally the us government paid  their tuition and fees  and they received a salary of thirty  dollars a month  hillsdale college was selected to host  one such program  and 195 of our young men enlisted in it  there was a barracks built right there  down manning street  in the old shoe factory the young men of  hillsdale lived in this barracks  engaged in drills parades and training  while also attending college classes  we have wonderful records in the  collegian of the goings-on  of course with the men busy in training  the collegian was mostly written by the  young women of our college  and we have many little notes about how  fine the men looked in their uniforms  and and how lonely the dining hall was  with all of the men gone  in the evenings the hillsdale women  would gather down the street to watch  the hillsdale men parade  for the lowering of the flag in the  retreat ceremony  and then return to their residences and  do what they have always done at  hillsdale college  surpassed the young men in their study  of latin and greek  but in 1918 we didn't only have a  world-rending conflict  of mind-boggling proportions we also saw  the terrible outbreak  of the infamous spanish influenza  hillsdale of course was not untouched by  the 1918  pandemic students became sick  and one of our students a young man in  the temporary barracks of the student  army training corps  died of the virus in december of that  year  he was harry kelly part of the class of  22  1922. he was 21 years old  and the collegian reports that he quote  rendered faithful  and efficient service in the student  army training court  i want to think about him for a moment  he walked  this same campus where we now sit  he saw these same hills i have trees in  my backyard  that are much older than he was and that  he might have seen  or sat under had he strolled past the  hungry howie's of yore onto norwood  avenue  why was he here what was he doing  why with the world falling apart in  disease  and war did a young man come to  hillsdale college  to enlist in the student army training  corps and study both  soldiery and the liberal arts why did he  do  this risking his very life which he duly  paid  why did the young women of the college  attend despite  the conflict and the influenza  why study latin or shakespeare or  physics at such a time  when there are so many more pressing and  practical concerns  our studies here point beyond and above  the quotidian mess of conflict and  disease  that is the constant companion of  mankind  it isn't that we ignore or dismiss the  suffering  that conflict and disease brings these  are real aspects of our existence  and we must exercise prudence and  fortitude in confronting them  as well i think as praying for the gifts  of faith hope and charity  but we must never be governed wholly by  them  to have one's actions finally determined  by conflict or disease  is to be subject to them to become slave  to them  the free man or woman the liberally  educated man or woman  always retains always the sense of that  which is higher  and finer those permanent things  those transcendent truths that rightly  order our existence  and give our lives meaning even in the  face of suffering  and so friends i can only end in  expressing my thanks and admiration  i am deeply grateful to our  administration  to our students and to the faculty and  staff  despite the tremendous headwinds we have  faced as a college  we continue to pursue our mission and  the great good  of the noble endeavor we are engaged in  with vigor  and courage i have never been so proud  to be a part of hillsdale college as in  this year  in these trying times thank you

